{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DCRCEixoNWmrEnv5WugwMj","type":"tool_result","content":"3:# Chapter 6: Fleets, Not Vessels\n4:## *\"Nobody sails one Pod\"*\n9:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the ~6% figure is this book's authored allocation, not a published CNCF weight. CNCF publishes four domain weights and twelve named competencies with no sub-weights. Match the exact disclosure phrasing used in the metadata lines of Chapters 2–5 before this ships. -->\n20:| §2 — A Loop You Can Watch Working | 7 min | Low | Anytime |\n22:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief pause |\n23:| §4 — Changing the Fleet Under Way | 18 min | **High** | Peak attention |\n25:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief pause |\n29:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief pause |\n30:| §9 — Nobody Sails One Pod (the chapter's ☀️ Zenith) | 4 min | Low | Anytime |\n38:**Recommended split point:** after ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2. Sections 1 through 5 are the Deployment arc from end to end. Sections 6 through 9 are the rest of the family and what the pattern turns into.\n40:*If you only have 15 minutes: read §1, jump to the decision tree at the close of §7, then work ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3. That is the highest exam-points-per-minute route through this chapter.*\n44:> *\"A standing order outlives the watch that received it. That is the whole reason you write it down.\"*\n45:> — Lodestar Ledgers\n49:## 🧭 Soundings\n61:5. A log-collection agent has to run on every machine in your fleet, including the machines that will be added next Tuesday. How would you express that requirement, as opposed to \"run six copies\"?\n76:3. **A list is exact but goes stale;** you must maintain it by hand every time membership changes. **A rule stays current** but can silently capture things you did not intend, because you never enumerated what it would match.\n80:5. **Express it as a property of the machine, not as a number.** \"One agent per host\" rather than \"six agents.\" The count is then whatever the fleet size happens to be, including next Tuesday's.\n84:7. **Desired state and current state.** When they match, the loop does nothing, and keeps watching. Doing nothing is a valid output of a control loop, not a pause in it.\n88:**If you got 6 or more right:** skim. Read the ★ Fixed Points, the ⚠ Navigational Hazards blocks, and §4 in full. The two rolling-update bounds and their defaults are the exam-checkable content, and they are easy to get backwards. Then work all three ☆ Taking Your Bearings checkpoints.\n92:**If you got 0 to 2 right:** read carefully, and take the recommended split after ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2. **If questions 7 and 8 were among your misses, re-read Chapter 3 §6 and Chapter 5 §4 before you start §2.** Those two sections are the debts this chapter collects, and §2 will read as a vocabulary drill without them.\n129:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §4 — a Pod is replaced, never rescheduled]* Chapter 5 handed you this question deliberately, and this is where it gets discharged. The Pod that vanished with its node is not recovered. Something notices it is gone and builds another one from a stored description.\n169:> You write the count on the Deployment. The Deployment sets it on the ReplicaSet it currently considers current. Both objects carry a `replicas` field [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24], but only one of them is the layer where the count is *acted on*, and that is the ReplicaSet.\n177:[Omitted long matching line]\n183:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** If you find yourself writing a bare Pod manifest for anything other than a one-off experiment, you have picked the wrong object. The question to ask is not \"how do I run this container\" but \"what should stay true about this container.\"\n187:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 14 §2 — a Helm chart's job is to template this object]*\n191:## ⚪ §2 — A Loop You Can Watch Working\n193:Chapter 3 promised you a control loop you could watch working in real time. This is it.\n195:[Omitted long matching line]\n197:Fill in the blanks and you have a ReplicaSet. Desired state is `.spec.replicas`. Current state is how many matching Pods actually exist. When they differ, the ReplicaSet creates or deletes Pods until they don't [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. That is Chapter 3's thermostat with the numbers filled in.\n199:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §6 — the control loop, and why nobody is in charge]*\n222:Nobody triggered that. No job ran. No alert fired and no runbook was consulted. A gap appeared between what you asked for and what existed, and a loop that had been watching the whole time closed it. The new Pod is not the old Pod recovered. It is a different Pod with a different name and a different UID *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §4 — replacement, not recovery]*, built from the same template.\n235:Now look at what the loop just did. It saw a gap of two between desired and current, and it closed it. That is precisely what it did sixty seconds ago when you deleted a Pod, except the gap that time was one and it appeared for a different reason. **The loop cannot tell the difference.** \"You asked for five and there are three\" and \"you asked for three and one died\" are the same input.\n241:One sentence is all the HPA gets here. Where its metrics come from and what else can autoscale are later problems. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §4 — metrics-server is what an HPA reads]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §3 — the autoscaling landscape]*\n245:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §1 — the Pod this loop just created still has to be placed on a node, and sometimes it can't be]*\n246:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — this churn is exactly why something needs a stable name]*\n256:[Omitted long matching line]\n266:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the outline (§3, Bearings #1 item 3) assumes a selector/template mismatch produces a live runaway in the cluster. All three cached sources state the API rejects the object outright. Section rewritten to teach the rejection with the runaway as the *reason* for it, and Bearings #1 item 3 adjusted to match. Please confirm this reading before the fact-accuracy audit. -->\n276:[Omitted long matching line]\n292:[Omitted long matching line]\n294:> What you observe is Pods appearing and disappearing on no schedule. What you look for, reasonably, is a crash loop or a failing probe or a node under pressure, because those are what flapping usually means. It is none of those. It is two standing orders written about the same crew.\n298:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §2 — a Service selects its backends with the same mechanism, which is a different controller reading the same labels]*\n299:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 §5 — deleting a workload does not delete everything it referenced]*\n303:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n316:**3.** ⚪ You write a Deployment manifest in which the Pod template's labels do not match the Deployment's selector, and you `kubectl apply` it. What happens?\n338:**2.** The ReplicaSet's desired count and its current count no longer agree, so it creates a Pod from its template to close the gap. **The correct answer turns on nothing having been triggered.** No scheduled task ran, no alert fired, no operator intervened. A loop that was already running observed a difference and acted on it, the same thing it does when you scale up, for the same reason [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n346:**4.** Desired state: `.spec.replicas`. Current state: the number of Pods currently matching the ReplicaSet's selector. Action: create or delete Pods until the two agree [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. If your answer for current state was \"the number of Pods it created,\" look again at §3. Membership is a query, and a Pod it never created can count toward the total.\n361:☐ How a running fleet gets replaced with a different one (next)\n366:## 🔵 §4 — Changing the Fleet Under Way\n376:A reader who has the ownership chain finds the rolling update obvious. A reader who does not finds it magic. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — the ownership chain]*\n404:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the outline's worked example (§4, fig02 spec, and Bearings #2 item 1) states the ceiling for 10 replicas at defaults is 12. The cached source specifies maxSurge rounds UP from a percentage, giving ceil(2.5) = 3 and a ceiling of 13. Draft uses 13 throughout, including fig02 and Bearings #2. Please confirm before the outline's number propagates to the Lodestar. -->\n451:[Omitted long matching line]\n463:This is the moment probes stop being a health-checking feature and become a release-safety mechanism. Chapter 5 told you a Pod that never reports ready never receives traffic. §4 tells you the larger consequence: a *version* that never reports ready never replaces the version that works. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §7 — liveness, readiness, and startup probes]*\n467:[Omitted long matching line]\n469:Rolling updates are the mechanism. There is a whole vocabulary of *release strategies* built on top of them: patterns with names, implemented with tooling that sits above the Deployment object. That vocabulary needs machinery this chapter has not introduced yet. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §4 — blue/green, canary, and A/B, and the tooling that implements them]*\n524:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 14 §5 — Helm rollback and Deployment rollback are different mechanisms wearing the same word]*\n525:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §5 — and a third thing, again wearing it]*\n527:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n539:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n541:Five questions on changing the fleet and what a change gets recorded as. One reaches back.\n580:After `progressDeadlineSeconds` (600 by default), the Deployment surfaces a `Progressing` condition with `reason: ProgressDeadlineExceeded` [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24]. Naming that signal is this chapter's job. Reading the events, finding which of the six causes fired, and fixing it is Chapter 13's. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §3]*\n593:🗺️ Chart → 🌊 **Passage** → 🌅 Dawn — you are past the halfway point, and past the hardest section.\n606:Now ask Soundings question 4 again. What if they are not interchangeable? What if this one is the primary and that one is the replica, each holding data that belongs to it specifically, each reachable at an address the others have written down?\n652:> **Extended Analogy:** A Deployment's Pods are a watch rotation. Any qualified hand can stand any watch; the roster says \"three on deck,\" not \"these three on deck,\" and when someone goes below you send up whoever is next. The order is a number and a qualification.\n654:> A StatefulSet's Pods are a pilot who knows this harbor. The order is not \"one pilot.\" It is *this* pilot, who has the chart of this specific approach, whose knowledge is not transferable by handing someone else the same job title. If that pilot is relieved, the relief has to be given the same chart, the same soundings, and the same name on the manifest, or the ship goes aground while everyone insists the roster is full.\n656:> Both are legitimate ways to staff a task. Confusing them costs you in exactly one direction: treat interchangeable crew as irreplaceable and you carry unnecessary machinery; treat irreplaceable crew as interchangeable and you lose data.\n664:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §4 — PersistentVolumes, claims, and how a Pod's storage follows its identity]*\n666:One more open thread, smaller: StatefulSets currently require a Headless Service to be responsible for the network identity of the Pods, and you are responsible for creating it [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24]. What a headless Service is belongs with the rest of Services. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §5 — headless Services and stable DNS names]*\n668:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 16 §3 — debugging StatefulSets and their claims]*\n670:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: chapter-01 line 435 carries a published cross-bearing reading \"*[see Ch 6 §3 — StatefulSets and stable identity]*\". StatefulSet is §6 in this chapter, and §3 is pinned by chapter-04 line 688 (selectors and ownership), which cannot move. Per the outline's Open Question #1, the recommended fix is a one-token edit in chapter-01: §3 → §6. Not fixable from inside this draft. -->\n686:Two of those come back later. Cluster networking plugins ship as DaemonSets *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §7 — CNI plugins and how Pod networking gets implemented]*, and node-level log agents are the canonical observability example *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §3 — node-level log collection]*.\n698:[Omitted long matching line]\n745:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n759:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §5 — a DaemonSet's Pods still go through scheduling, and taints are how a node opts out]*\n767:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: chapter-02 line 600 carries a published cross-bearing reading \"*[see Ch 6 §3 — CRDs and extending the API]*\". CRDs are §8 in this chapter; §3 is pinned by chapter-04 line 688 and cannot move, and CRDs cannot move earlier than §8 without teaching API extension before the reader has met a built-in controller. Per the outline's Open Question #1, the recommended fix is a one-token edit in chapter-02: §3 → §8. Not fixable from inside this draft. -->\n793:When you combine a custom resource with a custom controller, custom resources provide a true declarative API. The Kubernetes declarative API enforces a separation of responsibilities: you declare the desired state of your resource, and the controller keeps the current state of Kubernetes objects in sync with your declared desired state. This is in contrast to an imperative API, where you instruct a server what to do [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23].\n799:[Omitted long matching line]\n826:Four sockets, one pattern. Collecting them into a single statement about what kind of system Kubernetes is belongs later, when you have met all four in their own contexts. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §6 — CRI, CNI, CSI and CRDs, resolved into one story]*\n830:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 14 §6 — why Helm charts have a `crds/` directory]*\n831:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §3 — a delivery tool that is, structurally, a controller acting on custom resources]*\n835:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3\n893:## ☀️ §9 — Nobody Sails One Pod\n897:Take Chapter 3's control loop, the one you retrieved in Soundings question 7 and filled in for a ReplicaSet in ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1, and plug this chapter's controllers into it one at a time.\n908:                    │      CURRENT STATE        │\n935:And now the part that carries forward. That shape is not a Kubernetes implementation detail, a way the maintainers happened to build some controllers. It is what Kubernetes *is*. §8 already showed you that anyone can write a controller: read an object's `.spec`, do things, write its `.status` [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. There is no line in the architecture separating the loops Kubernetes ships from the loops you write.\n937:Which means the last question left is where the desired state lives. So far it has always lived in the cluster: an object you created with `kubectl apply`, stored by the API server, watched by a controller. There is no rule that says it has to. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §6 — a controller whose desired state lives in a Git repository, and why that is the same technology rather than a new one]*\n941:Which returns you to the title. Nobody sails one Pod, not because a single Pod is forbidden, and not because Pods are unimportant. A single Pod is a statement about right now. Every resource in this chapter is a statement about what should keep being true. That is the whole difference, and it is why you will stop reaching for `kubectl run` without ever making a decision to stop.\n998:**Q3.** 🔵 You submit a Deployment manifest whose `.spec.selector` does not match `.spec.template.metadata.labels`. What happens?\n1074:C) It deletes the current ReplicaSet and recreates Pods from a backup\n1136:B) A controller that watches `Certificate` objects and acts on them\n1146:C) Both run inside the API server; the difference is only which resources they watch\n1155:[Omitted long matching line]\n1189:[Omitted long matching line]\n1220:## The Voyage Ahead\n1230:You know how a fleet is described. Next you find out how a berth is assigned.\n1232:> *\"You can write the standing order in an afternoon. Finding the water to carry it out is the other half of the work.\"*\n1233:> — Lodestar Ledgers\n1237:## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1255:🗺️ Chart → 🌊 Passage → 🌅 **Dawn** — Part II's trunk is behind you."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"466f6b4a-7e79-4624-b651-f0067f3d5080","uuid":"74213d58-8ebe-446a-ac3b-92d9f6547eaf","timestamp":"2026-08-24T15:39:04.298Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"3:# Chapter 6: Fleets, Not Vessels\n4:## *\"Nobody sails one Pod\"*\n9:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the ~6% figure is this book's authored allocation, not a published CNCF weight. CNCF publishes four domain weights and twelve named competencies with no sub-weights. Match the exact disclosure phrasing used in the metadata lines of Chapters 2–5 before this ships. -->\n20:| §2 — A Loop You Can Watch Working | 7 min | Low | Anytime |\n22:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief pause |\n23:| §4 — Changing the Fleet Under Way | 18 min | **High** | Peak attention |\n25:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief pause |\n29:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief pause |\n30:| §9 — Nobody Sails One Pod (the chapter's ☀️ Zenith) | 4 min | Low | Anytime |\n38:**Recommended split point:** after ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2. Sections 1 through 5 are the Deployment arc from end to end. Sections 6 through 9 are the rest of the family and what the pattern turns into.\n40:*If you only have 15 minutes: read §1, jump to the decision tree at the close of §7, then work ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3. That is the highest exam-points-per-minute route through this chapter.*\n44:> *\"A standing order outlives the watch that received it. That is the whole reason you write it down.\"*\n45:> — Lodestar Ledgers\n49:## 🧭 Soundings\n61:5. A log-collection agent has to run on every machine in your fleet, including the machines that will be added next Tuesday. How would you express that requirement, as opposed to \"run six copies\"?\n76:3. **A list is exact but goes stale;** you must maintain it by hand every time membership changes. **A rule stays current** but can silently capture things you did not intend, because you never enumerated what it would match.\n80:5. **Express it as a property of the machine, not as a number.** \"One agent per host\" rather than \"six agents.\" The count is then whatever the fleet size happens to be, including next Tuesday's.\n84:7. **Desired state and current state.** When they match, the loop does nothing, and keeps watching. Doing nothing is a valid output of a control loop, not a pause in it.\n88:**If you got 6 or more right:** skim. Read the ★ Fixed Points, the ⚠ Navigational Hazards blocks, and §4 in full. The two rolling-update bounds and their defaults are the exam-checkable content, and they are easy to get backwards. Then work all three ☆ Taking Your Bearings checkpoints.\n92:**If you got 0 to 2 right:** read carefully, and take the recommended split after ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2. **If questions 7 and 8 were among your misses, re-read Chapter 3 §6 and Chapter 5 §4 before you start §2.** Those two sections are the debts this chapter collects, and §2 will read as a vocabulary drill without them.\n129:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §4 — a Pod is replaced, never rescheduled]* Chapter 5 handed you this question deliberately, and this is where it gets discharged. The Pod that vanished with its node is not recovered. Something notices it is gone and builds another one from a stored description.\n169:> You write the count on the Deployment. The Deployment sets it on the ReplicaSet it currently considers current. Both objects carry a `replicas` field [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24], but only one of them is the layer where the count is *acted on*, and that is the ReplicaSet.\n177:[Omitted long matching line]\n183:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** If you find yourself writing a bare Pod manifest for anything other than a one-off experiment, you have picked the wrong object. The question to ask is not \"how do I run this container\" but \"what should stay true about this container.\"\n187:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 14 §2 — a Helm chart's job is to template this object]*\n191:## ⚪ §2 — A Loop You Can Watch Working\n193:Chapter 3 promised you a control loop you could watch working in real time. This is it.\n195:[Omitted long matching line]\n197:Fill in the blanks and you have a ReplicaSet. Desired state is `.spec.replicas`. Current state is how many matching Pods actually exist. When they differ, the ReplicaSet creates or deletes Pods until they don't [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. That is Chapter 3's thermostat with the numbers filled in.\n199:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §6 — the control loop, and why nobody is in charge]*\n222:Nobody triggered that. No job ran. No alert fired and no runbook was consulted. A gap appeared between what you asked for and what existed, and a loop that had been watching the whole time closed it. The new Pod is not the old Pod recovered. It is a different Pod with a different name and a different UID *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §4 — replacement, not recovery]*, built from the same template.\n235:Now look at what the loop just did. It saw a gap of two between desired and current, and it closed it. That is precisely what it did sixty seconds ago when you deleted a Pod, except the gap that time was one and it appeared for a different reason. **The loop cannot tell the difference.** \"You asked for five and there are three\" and \"you asked for three and one died\" are the same input.\n241:One sentence is all the HPA gets here. Where its metrics come from and what else can autoscale are later problems. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §4 — metrics-server is what an HPA reads]* *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §3 — the autoscaling landscape]*\n245:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §1 — the Pod this loop just created still has to be placed on a node, and sometimes it can't be]*\n246:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — this churn is exactly why something needs a stable name]*\n256:[Omitted long matching line]\n266:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the outline (§3, Bearings #1 item 3) assumes a selector/template mismatch produces a live runaway in the cluster. All three cached sources state the API rejects the object outright. Section rewritten to teach the rejection with the runaway as the *reason* for it, and Bearings #1 item 3 adjusted to match. Please confirm this reading before the fact-accuracy audit. -->\n276:[Omitted long matching line]\n292:[Omitted long matching line]\n294:> What you observe is Pods appearing and disappearing on no schedule. What you look for, reasonably, is a crash loop or a failing probe or a node under pressure, because those are what flapping usually means. It is none of those. It is two standing orders written about the same crew.\n298:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §2 — a Service selects its backends with the same mechanism, which is a different controller reading the same labels]*\n299:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 §5 — deleting a workload does not delete everything it referenced]*\n303:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n316:**3.** ⚪ You write a Deployment manifest in which the Pod template's labels do not match the Deployment's selector, and you `kubectl apply` it. What happens?\n338:**2.** The ReplicaSet's desired count and its current count no longer agree, so it creates a Pod from its template to close the gap. **The correct answer turns on nothing having been triggered.** No scheduled task ran, no alert fired, no operator intervened. A loop that was already running observed a difference and acted on it, the same thing it does when you scale up, for the same reason [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n346:**4.** Desired state: `.spec.replicas`. Current state: the number of Pods currently matching the ReplicaSet's selector. Action: create or delete Pods until the two agree [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. If your answer for current state was \"the number of Pods it created,\" look again at §3. Membership is a query, and a Pod it never created can count toward the total.\n361:☐ How a running fleet gets replaced with a different one (next)\n366:## 🔵 §4 — Changing the Fleet Under Way\n376:A reader who has the ownership chain finds the rolling update obvious. A reader who does not finds it magic. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §1 — the ownership chain]*\n404:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the outline's worked example (§4, fig02 spec, and Bearings #2 item 1) states the ceiling for 10 replicas at defaults is 12. The cached source specifies maxSurge rounds UP from a percentage, giving ceil(2.5) = 3 and a ceiling of 13. Draft uses 13 throughout, including fig02 and Bearings #2. Please confirm before the outline's number propagates to the Lodestar. -->\n451:[Omitted long matching line]\n463:This is the moment probes stop being a health-checking feature and become a release-safety mechanism. Chapter 5 told you a Pod that never reports ready never receives traffic. §4 tells you the larger consequence: a *version* that never reports ready never replaces the version that works. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 §7 — liveness, readiness, and startup probes]*\n467:[Omitted long matching line]\n469:Rolling updates are the mechanism. There is a whole vocabulary of *release strategies* built on top of them: patterns with names, implemented with tooling that sits above the Deployment object. That vocabulary needs machinery this chapter has not introduced yet. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §4 — blue/green, canary, and A/B, and the tooling that implements them]*\n524:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 14 §5 — Helm rollback and Deployment rollback are different mechanisms wearing the same word]*\n525:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §5 — and a third thing, again wearing it]*\n527:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n539:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n541:Five questions on changing the fleet and what a change gets recorded as. One reaches back.\n580:After `progressDeadlineSeconds` (600 by default), the Deployment surfaces a `Progressing` condition with `reason: ProgressDeadlineExceeded` [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24]. Naming that signal is this chapter's job. Reading the events, finding which of the six causes fired, and fixing it is Chapter 13's. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §3]*\n593:🗺️ Chart → 🌊 **Passage** → 🌅 Dawn — you are past the halfway point, and past the hardest section.\n606:Now ask Soundings question 4 again. What if they are not interchangeable? What if this one is the primary and that one is the replica, each holding data that belongs to it specifically, each reachable at an address the others have written down?\n652:> **Extended Analogy:** A Deployment's Pods are a watch rotation. Any qualified hand can stand any watch; the roster says \"three on deck,\" not \"these three on deck,\" and when someone goes below you send up whoever is next. The order is a number and a qualification.\n654:> A StatefulSet's Pods are a pilot who knows this harbor. The order is not \"one pilot.\" It is *this* pilot, who has the chart of this specific approach, whose knowledge is not transferable by handing someone else the same job title. If that pilot is relieved, the relief has to be given the same chart, the same soundings, and the same name on the manifest, or the ship goes aground while everyone insists the roster is full.\n656:> Both are legitimate ways to staff a task. Confusing them costs you in exactly one direction: treat interchangeable crew as irreplaceable and you carry unnecessary machinery; treat irreplaceable crew as interchangeable and you lose data.\n664:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 11 §4 — PersistentVolumes, claims, and how a Pod's storage follows its identity]*\n666:One more open thread, smaller: StatefulSets currently require a Headless Service to be responsible for the network identity of the Pods, and you are responsible for creating it [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24]. What a headless Service is belongs with the rest of Services. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §5 — headless Services and stable DNS names]*\n668:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 16 §3 — debugging StatefulSets and their claims]*\n670:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: chapter-01 line 435 carries a published cross-bearing reading \"*[see Ch 6 §3 — StatefulSets and stable identity]*\". StatefulSet is §6 in this chapter, and §3 is pinned by chapter-04 line 688 (selectors and ownership), which cannot move. Per the outline's Open Question #1, the recommended fix is a one-token edit in chapter-01: §3 → §6. Not fixable from inside this draft. -->\n686:Two of those come back later. Cluster networking plugins ship as DaemonSets *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §7 — CNI plugins and how Pod networking gets implemented]*, and node-level log agents are the canonical observability example *[cross-bearing: see Ch 18 §3 — node-level log collection]*.\n698:[Omitted long matching line]\n745:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards**\n759:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §5 — a DaemonSet's Pods still go through scheduling, and taints are how a node opts out]*\n767:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: chapter-02 line 600 carries a published cross-bearing reading \"*[see Ch 6 §3 — CRDs and extending the API]*\". CRDs are §8 in this chapter; §3 is pinned by chapter-04 line 688 and cannot move, and CRDs cannot move earlier than §8 without teaching API extension before the reader has met a built-in controller. Per the outline's Open Question #1, the recommended fix is a one-token edit in chapter-02: §3 → §8. Not fixable from inside this draft. -->\n793:When you combine a custom resource with a custom controller, custom resources provide a true declarative API. The Kubernetes declarative API enforces a separation of responsibilities: you declare the desired state of your resource, and the controller keeps the current state of Kubernetes objects in sync with your declared desired state. This is in contrast to an imperative API, where you instruct a server what to do [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23].\n799:[Omitted long matching line]\n826:Four sockets, one pattern. Collecting them into a single statement about what kind of system Kubernetes is belongs later, when you have met all four in their own contexts. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §6 — CRI, CNI, CSI and CRDs, resolved into one story]*\n830:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 14 §6 — why Helm charts have a `crds/` directory]*\n831:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §3 — a delivery tool that is, structurally, a controller acting on custom resources]*\n835:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3\n893:## ☀️ §9 — Nobody Sails One Pod\n897:Take Chapter 3's control loop, the one you retrieved in Soundings question 7 and filled in for a ReplicaSet in ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1, and plug this chapter's controllers into it one at a time.\n908:                    │      CURRENT STATE        │\n935:And now the part that carries forward. That shape is not a Kubernetes implementation detail, a way the maintainers happened to build some controllers. It is what Kubernetes *is*. §8 already showed you that anyone can write a controller: read an object's `.spec`, do things, write its `.status` [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. There is no line in the architecture separating the loops Kubernetes ships from the loops you write.\n937:Which means the last question left is where the desired state lives. So far it has always lived in the cluster: an object you created with `kubectl apply`, stored by the API server, watched by a controller. There is no rule that says it has to. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 §6 — a controller whose desired state lives in a Git repository, and why that is the same technology rather than a new one]*\n941:Which returns you to the title. Nobody sails one Pod, not because a single Pod is forbidden, and not because Pods are unimportant. A single Pod is a statement about right now. Every resource in this chapter is a statement about what should keep being true. That is the whole difference, and it is why you will stop reaching for `kubectl run` without ever making a decision to stop.\n998:**Q3.** 🔵 You submit a Deployment manifest whose `.spec.selector` does not match `.spec.template.metadata.labels`. What happens?\n1074:C) It deletes the current ReplicaSet and recreates Pods from a backup\n1136:B) A controller that watches `Certificate` objects and acts on them\n1146:C) Both run inside the API server; the difference is only which resources they watch\n1155:[Omitted long matching line]\n1189:[Omitted long matching line]\n1220:## The Voyage Ahead\n1230:You know how a fleet is described. Next you find out how a berth is assigned.\n1232:> *\"You can write the standing order in an afternoon. Finding the water to carry it out is the other half of the work.\"*\n1233:> — Lodestar Ledgers\n1237:## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1255:🗺️ Chart → 🌊 Passage → 🌅 **Dawn** — Part II's trunk is behind you.","numLines":98,"totalLines":98}}
