{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CiCHkxKkJRtbbTREwAtYuo","type":"tool_result","content":"1189\t**Q19 — B.** Controllers are client programs that read and/or write the Kubernetes API, following a control loop: read an object's `.spec`, possibly do things, then update the object's `.status` [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. That description covers both. The difference is deployment, not architecture: the operator's controller normally runs outside the control plane, much as you would run any containerized application, for example as a Deployment [source: k8s-docs-operator-pattern-2026-08-23], while the built-in controllers ship with the control plane. **A** is wrong on both counts: operators are ordinary clients of the Kubernetes API [source: k8s-docs-operator-pattern-2026-08-23], not privileged processes. **C** is wrong; controllers talk *to* the API server, and a built-in controller sends messages to the API server that have useful side effects [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23] rather than running inside it. **D** invents a deployment model that would make no sense for either."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"466f6b4a-7e79-4624-b651-f0067f3d5080","uuid":"857d4cce-1087-4bfe-a96c-894c9fa77d26","timestamp":"2026-08-24T15:39:22.345Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-06\\draft-v1.md","content":"**Q19 — B.** Controllers are client programs that read and/or write the Kubernetes API, following a control loop: read an object's `.spec`, possibly do things, then update the object's `.status` [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. That description covers both. The difference is deployment, not architecture: the operator's controller normally runs outside the control plane, much as you would run any containerized application, for example as a Deployment [source: k8s-docs-operator-pattern-2026-08-23], while the built-in controllers ship with the control plane. **A** is wrong on both counts: operators are ordinary clients of the Kubernetes API [source: k8s-docs-operator-pattern-2026-08-23], not privileged processes. **C** is wrong; controllers talk *to* the API server, and a built-in controller sends messages to the API server that have useful side effects [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23] rather than running inside it. **D** invents a deployment model that would make no sense for either.","numLines":1,"startLine":1189,"totalLines":1255}}}
